# Claim 1

A method for interactively designing an engine and nacelle assembly, the method comprising:

configuring a fan section having a fan pressure ratio of less than 1.7;

configuring a compressor section in fluid communication with the fan section, the compressor section comprising a two-stage compressor arranged to provide an overall pressure ratio of the engine of greater than or equal to 35;

determining, via a computer processor, a plurality of design parameters associated with the fan section and the compressor section, the design parameters including at least one of a bypass ratio, a fan diameter, a core size, and a rotational speed relationship between the fan section and the compressor section;

generating, via the computer processor, a nacelle design model based at least in part on the plurality of design parameters, the nacelle design model defining a nacelle configured to enclose the fan section and to interact aerodynamically therewith;

displaying, via a user interface in communication with the computer processor, at least one representation of the engine and the nacelle design model;

receiving, via the user interface, a user input modifying at least one of the plurality of design parameters;

updating, via the computer processor, at least one of the fan section, the compressor section, and the nacelle design model in response to the user input, while maintaining the fan pressure ratio of less than 1.7 and the overall pressure ratio of greater than or equal to 35; and

outputting, via the computer processor, a final design configuration of the engine and nacelle assembly based on the updated fan section, compressor section, and nacelle design model.

# Patent Claim: Gas Turbine Engine Design Method

**Claim 1**

A method for designing a gas turbine engine having a fan section configured to split airflow between a core compressor and a bypass duct, the method comprising:

(a) defining a fan rotor having a plurality of fan blades arranged to receive incoming airflow along an engine longitudinal axis;

(b) configuring the fan section to divide the incoming airflow into a core airflow directed into a low pressure compressor and a bypass airflow directed into a bypass duct surrounding a core engine case, such that a ratio of the bypass airflow to the core airflow, measured at a design point operating condition, is at least 5:1;

(c) selecting a fan diameter and a fan blade quantity based on a target fan pressure ratio, wherein the fan pressure ratio is selected to be compatible with the bypass ratio of at least 5:1;

(d) designing a nacelle surrounding the fan section and defining an outer boundary of the bypass duct, said designing including:

  (i) determining a nacelle inlet lip geometry configured to provide a target inlet airflow distribution across the fan blades at a plurality of flight conditions;

  (ii) sizing a nacelle length-to-fan-diameter ratio to reduce nacelle drag while maintaining structural support for the fan section and a fan containment structure;

  (iii) contouring an inner barrel surface of the nacelle to define a bypass flow path geometry that maintains attached flow along the bypass duct at the design point operating condition;

  (iv) configuring a variable area fan nozzle at a downstream end of the nacelle, the variable area fan nozzle having an adjustable exit area configured to modulate a bypass duct exit pressure ratio across a range of engine operating conditions;

  (v) integrating a translating or pivoting thrust reverser structure into the nacelle, positioned aft of the fan section and configured to selectively redirect the bypass airflow;

(e) coupling the fan rotor to a low pressure turbine through a low pressure shaft, and interposing a gear reduction assembly between the fan rotor and the low pressure shaft, the gear reduction assembly configured to rotate the fan rotor at a lower rotational speed than the low pressure turbine, thereby enabling the fan diameter and bypass ratio selected in steps (b) and (c); and

(f) iteratively adjusting at least one of the fan pressure ratio, the nacelle inlet lip geometry, and the variable area fan nozzle exit area until a predetermined propulsive efficiency target is achieved at the design point operating condition while maintaining the bypass ratio at or above 5:1.

---

**Claim 2** (dependent)

The method of claim 1, wherein the gear reduction assembly comprises an epicyclic gear train having a gear ratio selected such that the fan rotor rotates at a speed less than 50% of a rotational speed of the low pressure turbine.

**Claim 3** (dependent)

The method of claim 1, wherein determining the nacelle inlet lip geometry in step (d)(i) comprises selecting an inlet lip contraction ratio configured to prevent flow separation during a crosswind ground operating condition while maintaining a low external drag profile during a cruise flight condition.

**Claim 4** (dependent)

The method of claim 1, further comprising selecting a fan blade sweep angle and a fan blade lean angle to reduce a fan noise signature while maintaining an aerodynamic loading distribution consistent with the bypass ratio of at least 5:1.

**Claim 5** (dependent)

The method of claim 1, wherein the bypass ratio is between 8:1 and 18:1, and wherein the fan pressure ratio selected in step (c) is between 1.20 and 1.55.

